{"id":264,"date":"2016-05-16T12:57:22","date_gmt":"2016-05-16T12:57:22","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/masterbadminton.dev\/malaysian-open-super-series-2011-quarter-final\/"},"modified":"2016-09-01T14:21:57","modified_gmt":"2016-09-01T14:21:57","slug":"malaysia-open-2011-leechongwei-nguyentienminh-html","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/masterbadminton.com\/malaysia-open-2011-leechongwei-nguyentienminh.html","title":{"rendered":"Malaysian Open Super Series 2011 QUARTER FINAL"},"content":{"rendered":"<div id=\"ContentColumn\">\n<div class=\"Liner\">\n<h1 style=\"text-align: center;\">Lee Chong Wei vs Nguyen Tien Minh<\/h1>\n<div style=\"float: left;\"><\/div>\n<p>Watch the Malaysia Open Super Series 2011 QUARTER FINALS, between Lee Chong Wei and Nguyen Tien Minh.<\/p>\n<p>Chong Wei was not in good form during the first game. Tien was leading at the start but because of a couple of mistakes, Chong Wei managed to chase the points.<\/p>\n<p>In the second game, we can observe a significant change of pace by Chong Wei. He was playing at a much faster pace in game 2. Tien had to move faster and harder to retrieve the shuttles from Chong Wei.<\/p>\n<p>Chong Wei was able to deliver a series of strong attacks at a faster pace, which helped him won a couple of rallies.<\/p>\n<p>However, Tien was able to keep up with the faster pace after a couple of rallies. In fact, he could still &#8216;out-pace&#8217; Chong Wei sometimes. Tien is more of a physical player whose qualities lies on his speed and strength.<\/p>\n<p>There was a controversial call made at around 8:58 of the 3rd video, which was at Chong Wei&#8217;s disadvantage. I think a clear error has been made (watch the slow motion replay) but the umpire did not overrule.<\/p>\n<p>This call had indeed fired up Chong Wei. Probably because he was emotionally affected, he was playing more aggressively and performing better, at an even faster game tempo.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<div align=\"CENTER\"><strong>Part 1<\